  ],
  "claims": [
    {
      "claim": "The query is an N-propyl ajmaline (prajmalium) quaternary ammonium alkaloid; the most transferable direct low-F outcomes are ipratropium (2.0-3.27% absolute F, low partly due to intestinal secretion), physostigmine (1-5%, 3% oral solution), lisuride (~10%, 10-20%), and pentazocine (18%, 0.18±0.05), all affirmatively below 20%.",
      "card_ids": ["C02","C03","C26","C16","C01","C10","C23","C36","C12","C15","C39","C07","C14","C33","C35"]
    },
    {
      "claim": "L6 C85 shows ajmaline (the parent scaffold) has a rate-dependent hepatic extraction ratio in rats, with higher infusion rates yielding smaller extraction; this is mechanistic evidence for hepatic first-pass loss in the ajmaline scaffold and supports low F for the N-propyl quaternary query.",
      "card_ids": ["C85"]
    },
    {
      "claim": "The remaining L6 hepatic-clearance/metabolic-stability records concern desipramine, mianserin, imipramine, perazine, and meperidine and are not query-relevant; they do not provide affirmative evidence for F>=20%.",
      "card_ids": ["C78","C79","C84","C87","C82","C83","C88","C80","C77","C81","C86"]
    },
    {
      "claim": "Physostigmine values of 23% and 25.3% are higher outliers but are outweighed by the consistent 1-5% and 3% absolute F measurements.",
      "card_ids": ["C22","C40"]
    }
  ],
  "new_evidence_assessment": [
    {
      "family": "hepatic_clearance_metabolic_stability",
      "applicability": "moderate",
      "direction": "supportive",
      "decision_effect": "strengthened",
      "card_ids": ["C85"]
    }
  ],
  "evidence_gaps": [
    "No direct experimental oral bioavailability measurement is available for the query molecule (N-propyl ajmaline/prajmalium).",
    "No query-specific hepatic clearance or metabolic stability data are available; C85 is for the ajmaline parent in rats, not the quaternary N-propyl derivative.",
    "The explicit [N@+] quaternary ammonium in the SMILES conflicts with the computed neutral fraction of 0.98; if the permanent cation is real, the favorable logD/TPSA-based prior is not reliable.",
    "Transfer from ipratropium, physostigmine, lisuride, pentazocine, and ajmaline is analog-based; species, formulation, dose, and route differences limit quantitative extrapolation.",
    "C85 does not provide a numeric extraction ratio or oral F value; rate dependence alone does not quantify first-pass loss.",
    "Other L6 records are for unrelated chemotypes and do not bear on the query."
  ],
  "decision_summary": "Prediction remains low. The query is an N-propyl ajmaline (prajmalium) quaternary ammonium alkaloid; direct low-F analog outcomes (ipratropium 2-3%, physostigmine 1-5%/3%, lisuride ~10%, pentazocine 18%) affirmatively support F<20%. L6 C85 adds mechanistic evidence that the ajmaline parent scaffold undergoes rate-dependent hepatic extraction in rats, supporting hepatic first-pass loss; no L6 record contradicts the low prediction. Confidence remains moderate because no direct query-specific F or clearance data exist and the explicit quaternary charge conflicts with the computed neutral fraction."
}

